# Button Input System Implementation

## Problem Description

You are tasked with implementing a button input management system for a game engine. The system should track the state of buttons and detect when specific combinations of buttons are pressed simultaneously (combos). 

The system consists of two main components:
1. `Button` class - tracks the state and duration of a single button press
2. `InputManager` class - manages multiple buttons and detects button combos

## Class Requirements

### Button Class
```cpp
class Button {
public:
    enum class State { RELEASED, PRESSED, HELD, JUST_RELEASED };

    Button();

    void updateFrame();
    void press();
    void release();

    State getState() const;
    int getFramesHeld() const;
    bool isPressed() const;
    bool isHeld() const;
    bool isReleased() const;

private:
    State m_state;
    int m_framesHeld;
};
```

### InputManager Class
```cpp
class InputManager {
public:
    InputManager();

    void updateAll();
    void addButton(const string& name);
    void pressButton(const string& name);
    void releaseButton(const string& name);

    Button::State getButtonState(const string& name) const;
    int getButtonFramesHeld(const string& name) const;

    void addButtonCombo(const vector<string>& buttons, 
                       const string& comboName, 
                       function<void()> callback);
    void checkCombos();

private:
    struct Combo {
        vector<string> buttons;
        function<void()> callback;
        bool wasTriggered;
    };

    unordered_map<string, Button> m_buttons;
    unordered_map<string, Combo> m_combos;
};
```

## Behavior Specifications

### Button States
- `RELEASED`: Button is not pressed
- `PRESSED`: Button was just pressed (first frame)
- `HELD`: Button is being held down (subsequent frames after PRESSED)
- `JUST_RELEASED`: Button was just released (first frame after release)

### Button Transitions
1. When pressed from RELEASED state → becomes PRESSED (framesHeld = 1)
2. On next updateFrame() while PRESSED → becomes HELD (framesHeld increments)
3. When released from PRESSED or HELD → becomes JUST_RELEASED
4. On next updateFrame() while JUST_RELEASED → becomes RELEASED (framesHeld = 0)

### InputManager Operations
1. `addButton`: Register a new button with given name
2. `pressButton`/`releaseButton`: Change button state
3. `updateAll`: Advance all buttons' states by one frame
4. `addButtonCombo`: Register a combo that triggers when all specified buttons are pressed/held
5. `checkCombos`: Check all registered combos and trigger callbacks if conditions are met

## Example Usage

```cpp
InputManager inputs;

// Register buttons
inputs.addButton("Jump");
inputs.addButton("Fire");
inputs.addButton("Special");

// Register a combo
inputs.addButtonCombo({"Jump", "Fire"}, "JumpFire", []() {
    cout << "Jump+Fire combo activated!" << endl;
});

// Simulate game loop
for (int frame = 0; frame < 10; frame++) {
    if (frame == 1) inputs.pressButton("Jump");
    if (frame == 2) inputs.pressButton("Fire");

    inputs.updateAll();
    inputs.checkCombos();

    if (frame == 5) inputs.releaseButton("Jump");
}
```

## Constraints

1. All class and member names must match exactly as specified
2. Button state transitions must follow the specified rules
3. Combos should only trigger once when all buttons are first pressed/held
4. Combos should be able to trigger again after being released and re-pressed
5. Non-existent buttons should be handled gracefully (return RELEASED state)

## Evaluation Criteria

Your implementation will be tested against the following scenarios:
1. Basic button press and state transitions
2. Multiple frame updates with held buttons
3. Button release scenarios
4. Single and multiple button combos
5. Combo triggering with held buttons
6. Handling of non-existent buttons

Note: You should only submit the implementation of the two classes. Do not include any test code or main function.

