# 3D Player Movement Simulation

## Problem Description
Implement a 3D player movement system with physics, input handling, and collision detection. The system should simulate a player moving in a 3D environment with gravity, jumping, and obstacle avoidance.

## Class Requirements

### Vector3 Class
```cpp
class Vector3 {
public:
    float x, y, z;

    Vector3(float x = 0, float y = 0, float z = 0);
    Vector3 operator+(const Vector3& other) const;
    Vector3 operator-(const Vector3& other) const;
    Vector3 operator*(float scalar) const;
    float magnitude() const;
    Vector3 normalized() const;
};
```

### InputSystem Class
```cpp
class InputSystem {
private:
    std::map<int, bool> keyStates;

public:
    void setKeyState(int key, bool state);
    bool getKey(int key) const;
};
```

### Player Class
```cpp
class Player {
private:
    Vector3 position;
    Vector3 velocity;
    Vector3 acceleration;
    bool isGrounded;
    float moveSpeed;
    float jumpForce;
    float gravity;
    float groundY;
    std::vector<Vector3> obstacles;

public:
    Player();
    void Update(float deltaTime, const InputSystem& input);
    Vector3 GetPosition() const;
    Vector3 GetVelocity() const;
    bool IsGrounded() const;
    void AddObstacle(const Vector3& obstacle);
};
```

## Input/Output Specifications

### Input
- The system will receive keyboard inputs mapped to integers:
  - 1: Move left
  - 2: Move right
  - 3: Move up (forward in 3D space)
  - 4: Move down (backward in 3D space)
  - 5: Jump
- Time delta between updates (in seconds)

### Output
- The player's position (Vector3) after each update
- The player's velocity (Vector3) after each update
- Whether the player is grounded (bool)

## Constraints
- All movement calculations must use proper physics with acceleration and velocity
- Diagonal movement should be normalized
- Jumping should only work when grounded
- Collision with obstacles should stop movement
- Player should not fall below ground level (y=0)

## Example Usage
```cpp
int main() {
    InputSystem input;
    Player player;

    // Move right for 1 second
    input.setKeyState(2, true);
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        player.Update(0.1f, input);
        Vector3 pos = player.GetPosition();
        cout << "Position: (" << pos.x << ", " << pos.y << ", " << pos.z << ")\n";
    }

    // Jump
    input.setKeyState(5, true);
    player.Update(0.1f, input);
    input.setKeyState(5, false);
    cout << "After jump - Y position: " << player.GetPosition().y << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

## Notes
- You must implement all classes exactly as specified
- The physics simulation should be frame-rate independent (use deltaTime)
- Obstacles have a collision radius of 1.5 units
- Gravity is -9.8 m/s² by default
- Movement speed is 5 units/second by default
- Jump force is 7 units by default

