# Item-Based Collaborative Filtering System

## Problem Description
Implement a recommendation system that uses item-based collaborative filtering to predict user ratings for items they haven't rated yet. The system should:
1. Maintain a user-item rating matrix
2. Calculate item similarities using cosine similarity
3. Generate rating predictions based on similar items

## Class Requirements
You need to implement the following classes and functions exactly as specified:

### RatingMatrix Class
```cpp
class RatingMatrix {
private:
    map<string, map<string, double>> userItemRatings;
    map<string, map<string, double>> itemUserRatings;

public:
    // Add a rating to the matrix
    void addRating(const string& user, const string& item, double rating);

    // Get user-item ratings
    const map<string, map<string, double>>& getUserItemRatings() const;

    // Get item-user ratings (transposed matrix)
    const map<string, map<string, double>>& getItemUserRatings() const;

    // Get all items
    vector<string> getAllItems() const;

    // Get all users
    vector<string> getAllUsers() const;
};
```

### Required Functions
```cpp
// Calculates cosine similarity between two vectors
double cosineSimilarity(const map<string, double>& vec1, const map<string, double>& vec2);

// Finds similar items using cosine similarity
map<string, vector<pair<string, double>>> findSimilarItems(
    const RatingMatrix& matrix, 
    int k, 
    double similarityThreshold = 0.0);

// Predicts ratings using item-item collaborative filtering
map<string, map<string, double>> predictRatings(
    const RatingMatrix& matrix,
    const map<string, vector<pair<string, double>>>& similarItems,
    int k = 5);
```

## Input/Output Specifications
- The system should accept user-item ratings through the `addRating()` method
- `findSimilarItems()` should return top-k most similar items for each item
- `predictRatings()` should return predicted ratings for unrated items

## Constraints
1. User and item IDs are strings
2. Ratings are double values
3. k must be a positive integer
4. Similarity threshold must be ≥ 0.0
5. All functions must handle empty inputs gracefully

## Example Usage
```cpp
RatingMatrix matrix;
matrix.addRating("Alice", "Movie1", 5.0);
matrix.addRating("Alice", "Movie2", 3.0);
matrix.addRating("Bob", "Movie1", 4.0);
matrix.addRating("Bob", "Movie3", 2.0);
matrix.addRating("Charlie", "Movie2", 1.0);
matrix.addRating("Charlie", "Movie3", 4.0);

// Find top 2 similar items for each item
auto similarItems = findSimilarItems(matrix, 2);

// Predict ratings for all unrated user-item pairs
auto predictions = predictRatings(matrix, similarItems);

// predictions will contain estimated ratings for:
// - Alice's rating for Movie3
// - Bob's rating for Movie2
// - Charlie's rating for Movie1
```

## Notes
1. Implement all classes and functions exactly as specified
2. Do not modify the function signatures
3. Handle edge cases appropriately (empty inputs, no similar items, etc.)
4. The cosine similarity of two vectors should be between 0 and 1
5. When predicting ratings, only consider items the user hasn't rated yet

Your solution will be evaluated based on:
1. Correct implementation of the specified classes and functions
2. Accuracy of similarity calculations and rating predictions
3. Proper handling of edge cases
4. Code organization and readability

