# Advanced 2D Game Player System

## Problem Description
You are tasked with implementing a player system for a 2D game that handles movement, shooting, health management, and scoring. The player should be able to move within defined boundaries, shoot projectiles, take damage, heal, and accumulate score points.

## Class Requirements
Implement a class `AdvancedPlayer` with the following exact specifications:

```cpp
struct PlayerState {
    float position_x;
    float position_y;
    bool alive;
    bool shooting;
    float speed;
    int health;
    int score;
    vector<pair<float, float>> projectiles;
};

class AdvancedPlayer {
private:
    PlayerState state;
    float boundary_left;
    float boundary_right;
    float boundary_top;
    float boundary_bottom;
    float projectile_speed;
    int max_health;

public:
    AdvancedPlayer(float start_x, float start_y, 
                  float left_bound, float right_bound,
                  float top_bound, float bottom_bound,
                  int initial_health = 100);

    void processMovement(float delta_time, bool move_left, bool move_right, bool move_up, bool move_down);
    void processShooting(float delta_time, bool shoot_command);
    void takeDamage(int damage);
    void addScore(int points);
    void heal(int amount);
    const PlayerState& getState() const;
    vector<pair<float, float>> getActiveProjectiles() const;
};
```

## Function Specifications

### Constructor
- Initializes player position, boundaries, and health
- Sets initial state: alive, not shooting, speed 0, score 0
- Projectile speed is fixed at 500 units/second

### processMovement
- Updates player position based on movement commands and delta time
- Handles diagonal movement normalization (speed should be consistent)
- Enforces movement boundaries
- Updates current movement speed in state

### processShooting
- Creates new projectiles when shooting command is activated
- Updates positions of all active projectiles
- Removes projectiles that leave the top boundary
- Tracks shooting state to prevent continuous firing

### takeDamage
- Reduces health by specified amount
- Sets player to dead (alive=false) if health reaches 0

### addScore
- Increases player score by specified points

### heal
- Increases health by specified amount, without exceeding max health
- Has no effect if player is dead

### getState
- Returns current player state (position, health, score, etc.)

### getActiveProjectiles
- Returns vector of active projectile positions

## Constraints
- All position values are floats
- Movement speed is 300 units/second in any direction
- Diagonal movement speed should be normalized (300 * 1/sqrt(2))
- Projectiles move upward at 500 units/second
- Health cannot exceed maximum health when healing
- Player cannot move outside specified boundaries

## Example Usage
```cpp
AdvancedPlayer player(400.0f, 500.0f, 0.0f, 800.0f, 0.0f, 600.0f);

// Move player diagonally up-left for 1 second
player.processMovement(1.0f, true, false, true, false);

// Shoot and let projectiles move for 0.5 seconds
player.processShooting(0.5f, true);

// Take damage and heal
player.takeDamage(30);
player.heal(10);

// Add score points
player.addScore(50);

// Get current state
PlayerState state = player.getState();

// Get active projectiles
auto projectiles = player.getActiveProjectiles();
```

## Notes
- Your implementation must exactly match the specified class structure
- Pay attention to boundary conditions and edge cases
- All movement and projectile calculations should use floating-point arithmetic
- The test cases will verify all functionality including movement, shooting, damage, healing, and scoring systems

